      "name": "Local Computation of the Rank of Apparition via Multiplicative Order",
      "description": "By the Apparition-Order Bridge, the rank of apparition of a prime p in the sequence b^n - 1 equals the multiplicative order of b in the residue field Z/pZ. This algorithm computes it locally by iterating the power b^k modulo p until it returns to 1, never forming the exponentially large integers b^n - 1. By Fermat descent the loop terminates within p - 1 steps; each step is a modular multiplication on integers below p^2, giving O(entryPoint * log p) bit complexity.",
      "pseudocode": "function MersenneEntryPoint(b, p):\n    require p prime and (b mod p) != 0\n    x <- b mod p\n    acc <- x ; k <- 1\n    while acc != 1:\n        acc <- (acc * x) mod p\n        k <- k + 1\n    return k          # = order of b mod p = rank of apparition",
      "code": "from math import gcd\nfrom typing import Optional\n\n\ndef is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Deterministic trial-division primality test.\"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return n == 2\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        if n % d == 0:\n            return False\n        d += 2\n    return True\n\n\ndef mersenne_entry_point(b: int, p: int) -> Optional[int]:\n    \"\"\"Compute entryPoint(b^n - 1, p) = orderOf(b mod p) for prime p with p not dividing b.\n\n    By the Apparition-Order Bridge the rank of apparition equals the\n    multiplicative order of b in the residue field Z/pZ, so we never form the\n    exploding integers b^n - 1: we iterate the power on the finite clock mod p.\n    By Fermat descent the loop runs at most p - 1 times.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not is_prime(p) or b % p == 0:\n        return None\n    x = b % p\n    acc = x\n    k = 1\n    while acc != 1 % p:\n        acc = (acc * x) % p\n        k += 1\n    return k  # equals the order of b mod p, hence the entry point\n"
