      "name": "Fermat-Descent Entry Point via Divisor Enumeration",
      "description": "A faster variant of the entry-point computation exploiting Fermat descent: the order of b modulo p divides p - 1, so the rank of apparition is the least divisor d of p - 1 with (b mod p)^d = 1. Enumerating divisors of p - 1 in ascending order and testing each with fast modular exponentiation yields O(d(p-1) * log p) work, far below the naive order-many multiplications when the order is large.",
      "pseudocode": "function MersenneEntryPointFast(b, p):\n    require p prime and (b mod p) != 0\n    x <- b mod p\n    for d in ascending divisors of (p - 1):\n        if pow(x, d, p) == 1:\n            return d       # least such d is the order\n    return undefined",
      "code": "from typing import List, Optional\n\n\ndef divisors(m: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"All positive divisors of m, ascending.\"\"\"\n    small, large = [], []\n    d = 1\n    while d * d <= m:\n        if m % d == 0:\n            small.append(d)\n            if d != m // d:\n                large.append(m // d)\n        d += 1\n    return small + large[::-1]\n\n\ndef mersenne_entry_point_fast(b: int, p: int) -> Optional[int]:\n    \"\"\"Entry point of p in b^n - 1 via Fermat descent: the order divides p - 1,\n    so it is the least divisor d of p - 1 with (b mod p)^d = 1 (mod p).\n\n    Each test is a modular exponentiation in O(log d) multiplications, so the\n    whole routine costs O(d(p-1) * log p) rather than O(order) naive steps.\n    \"\"\"\n    if p < 2 or b % p == 0:\n        return None\n    x = b % p\n    for d in divisors(p - 1):          # ascending; first hit is the order\n        if pow(x, d, p) == 1 % p:\n            return d\n    return None\n"
